nd the action is at its  highest in this part  thankfully one of the reasons i love how  this entire story expands and progresses  is largely due to a concept in art which  is called  context drives form it's a simple idea  and what that means in a nutshell is  that the events from the past inform  and help design the outcomes later in  other words the wedding goes bad  therefore  big mom chases the straw hats therefore  her forces attack the straw hats  therefore luffy is forced to improvise  and overcome everything works as a  building block informed from the prior  events  and this is just one example of how and  why the plot works and flows so well  in this portion of the manga for me  however it's not without something i  took issue with you see there's a  specific callback to the bomb luffy  accidentally sent to big mom  in a box now when this was set up way  back at the end of fishman island  i was very excited this is quite  literally alfred hitchcock's bomb under  the table analogy in action across a  detailed and sprawling world  simply put if you put a bomb under the  table and say it's going to go off  there's a tension that's established  between the story  and its audience and ever since that  bomb was put in motion i had anticipated  it showing up at any moment  coating the pages of every interaction  with big mom was a palpable tension  concerning when the bomb might show up  and go off i was certain that it might  inform an action by big mom towards  luffy or perhaps it might even show up  and the straw hats might leverage it for  their own game  but none of this happens however instead  the straw hats for the first time that i  can think of were literally beaten and  about to lose without  any options this is when through sheer  coincidence  in circumstances surrounding the bomb's  explosion it indirectly saved the straw  hat crew to me that is extremely  convenient and slightly disappointing  considering it was something that was  left lying there