e most  consistently engaging and fun characters  to watch trevors is seen with  and in the same way arlen park served to  make nami a more complex and interesting  character i sincerely believe whole cake  island did exactly that for sanji and  i'm genuinely  happy about that that coupled with the  shocking visuals of big mom as she  shrinks into a more  imposing skinnier version of herself in  pursuit of the cake  is both haunting and awesome in short  this arc made for an exceptionally well  thought out chase sequence rescue and  escape story now as many of you might be  wondering  if i have all of those positive things  to say about this arc and with it being  one of my favorite stories from the  series so far  how on earth could it be that there is  something about this that frustrates me  more than anything else in the series  well in short the more you like  something the more it has a chance to  frustrate and disappoint you the last  chapter of this arc is chapter 902  and i honestly think it's one of the  best chapters from one piece in the  post-time skip material it doesn't do  anything amazing mind you it's heartfelt  and in places quite funny but so what a  lot of one piece is like that in fact  most of it is  and i agree completely with those  arguments but when i see this chapter i  honestly got a little frustrated because  its paneling is fantastic every single  page is clear  easy to read and above all else composed  beautifully  and creatively with instances like  pudding's kiss to sanji being  illustrated like a film reel this is  exactly like how oda drew and structured  his pages back in the early days of one  piece  and it was this reading of this art  coupled with a conversation i had with  another fan of the series that informed  me  that if oda were to structure his  stories like this  consistently which he has shown that he  can do with these modern  arcs we wouldn't be nearly as far into  the story as we would be  which to me isn't a reason not to do it  personally as a fan i can understand why  oda wouldn't want to do it  professionally but for me as a fan  it really disappoints me which might  explain why some people like the animes  adaptation more than the manga  if all you're trying to do is cramming  your chapters with tiny panels in an  effort to get as much story completed as  possible  while you are delivering more story  technically you are crushing the quality  of the way you are telling that story  one criminal offense in this act that i  noticed was that i'm jinbe's retirement  from big mom's pirates  this should have been a large panel  front and center on the page but instead  it was surrounded by tiny panels all in  an effort to get as much of the story  across as possible in a short a span of  time in saying this i know that this  doesn't bother everyone and it might not  even bother a lot of you and i can  totally understand that oda doesn't want  to do this for the rest of his life but  it still irks me to know that oda can  produce beautifully elegant and  well-structured panels but still  chooses not to do in an effort to cram  as much story in as possible this is  like someone speed reading a bedtime  story to you as a kid  sure you get more story but it's not  nearly as engaging or enjoyable at least  not for me  and the very fact that one piece is as  impossibly engaging and amazing as it is  while in my opinion not telling the  story as well as it could be is both a  strange thing to come and realize but  also for me a little frustrating i still  love one piece but this is i think a  valid criticism i totally respect if you  love it purely for the story but for me  manga is a visual medium as much as it  is a written one and the visuals are  just as important  to me as the story i want it also to be  understood that while i have bulldozed  my way through this series with the same  reckless abandoned luffy did when  storming the gates of annie's lobby i  haven't lost sight of 